# Line endings and binary handling.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on at least one clone of this repo, and with no rules
# here Git decides text-vs-binary by sniffing each file. It guessed wrong twice,
# both in ways that only surface somewhere else:
#
#   docs/launch-checklist.pdf committed with "LF will be replaced by CRLF" -
#   line-ending translation applied to a PDF. The blob went in intact, but a
#   fresh clone would have had every 0x0A in it rewritten on checkout, and the
#   file would no longer open.
#
#   scripts/backup.sh and deploy/live-push.sh are stored LF and correct, but
#   checked out with CRLF on Windows. DEPLOY.md offers "rsync the project up"
#   as an alternative to cloning on the droplet, and a shell script copied that
#   way runs on Linux with a trailing \r on every line - the shebang fails with
#   a bare "not found", and backup.sh dies on its first command under
#   set -euo pipefail, nightly, in a cron job whose output nobody reads.
#
# Declaring them is the fix. The heuristic is not something to rely on for
# files this repo commits deliberately and then ships to a server.

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf

*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.db    binary
